│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ split_cry_reverse_chain                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The second program achieved the best score (0.91) with 50 parameters by splitting the     │
│                          │ reverse chain into odd and even parameter variants (crz_rev_a and crz_rev_b). The current │
│                          │ program simplified this to a single cry_rev parameter, which reduced the score to 0.90.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This edit restores the beneficial parameter-splitting strategy while keeping CRY gates    │
│                          │ (instead of reverting to CRZ). The key insight is that odd-indexed and even-indexed       │
│                          │ reverse links ([7→6], [5→4], [3→2], [1→0] vs [6→5], [4→3], [2→1]) may benefit from        │
│                          │ different learned parameters.                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ By using two parameters instead of one, we provide the model with richer expressivity     │
│                          │ while maintaining parameter efficiency (only adding 1 parameter). This should tighten the │
│                          │ generalization gap and improve validation/test accuracy without significantly increasing  │
│                          │ training time or depth.                                                                   │
